# Protections for the predictable-PRNG-secret variant corpus. Held IDENTICAL
# across every target in this family on purpose: the *source of the secret* is
# the only variable, so a protection difference would confound the measurement.
#
# This category is not a memory-corruption category -- nothing in the solve path
# writes out of bounds -- so no protection here gates the technique. That makes
# the reason for each flag different from the usual one: these are pinned so
# that the executor's DERIVATION reads the same kind of image on every target.
#
# -fno-stack-protector  Irrelevant to the solve path (there is no overflow), but
#                       pinned so the family is uniform and a later reader is not
#                       left wondering whether a canary mattered. It also keeps
#                       the disassembly of main free of the __stack_chk_fail
#                       epilogue that would otherwise sit between the srand()
#                       call and the comparison the analysis walks.
# -no-pie               Fixed addresses, so the `call <srand@plt>` /
#                       `call <time@plt>` edges the seed-source derivation
#                       matches on are plain absolute call targets objdump can
#                       name, rather than PIE-relative thunks.
# -O0                   The seed source is derived from the instruction stream:
#                       `call time@plt` (or getpid@plt) whose result feeds
#                       `call srand@plt`, and the literal base of strtoul().
#                       At -O1 and above gcc constant-folds the fixed-seed case,
#                       hoists the time() call, and can turn the modulus into a
#                       reciprocal multiply with no recoverable divisor -- at
#                       which point the target has silently stopped being the
#                       program this corpus documents.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT -static. The gate derives applicability from rand/random/
# rand_r/srand appearing as DYNAMIC IMPORTS (a PLT entry with a name), and the
# seed source from named `call <fn@plt>` edges. A statically linked image has no
# PLT and no dynamic symbols, so the same derivation would have to fall back to
# matching local symbol names -- i.e. it would be measuring a different
# inference than the one this executor claims to make.
-fno-stack-protector
-no-pie
-O0
